{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ yeltsin \"foreign policy\" (+ asia africa \"latin america\"))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_64318028#1_134618485","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q912","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_64318028#1_134618485\" , \"end_char\": 2257.000000 , \"headings\": \"Main Trends of Russia’s Foreign Policy in Transforming East and Southeast Asia Main Trends of Russia’s Foreign Policy in Transforming East and Southeast Asia Changing calculations in East <cover>Asia The Vietnam question Developments in Russia’s foreign policy Vladimir N. Kolotov \" , \"segment\": \"According to official statistics, under Yeltsin </cover>Russia’s GDP declined by roughly 60% – which is unprecedented in peace time – with all the ensuing consequences. Russia became weak and concentrated upon domestic problems, and by 1998 it had lost its erstwhile role and almost all influence in East Asia. Under Vladimir Putin, Russia managed to match its own GDP of 1990 only in 2006! Putin proved effective at stopping political and economic chaos, soothing armed conflicts inside Russia, and restoring a level of social and economic development. As a result, Russia has returned to the world stage as a strong state. It is expected that new president Dmitry Medvedev will continue that political course and that the energy dimension will "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"18/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_64318028#1_134618485","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage gives direct supporting evidence that under Yeltsin Russia became weak, focused on domestic problems, and by 1998 had lost almost all influence in East Asia, a region containing non-aligned states such as Vietnam. It is not explicitly dedicated to non-aligned countries as a group.","learned":"A strong broader effect was Yeltsin-era Russia's economic collapse and inward focus, causing loss of influence in East Asia; Vietnam may be a promising non-aligned-country case."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_64318028#1_134618485","reason":"The snippet is from a focused Russia foreign-policy article with a Vietnam section and may contain direct evidence on Yeltsin-era effects on a non-aligned country or region."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_64318028#1_134618485"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_64318028#1_134618485","document":"Main Trends of Russia’s Foreign Policy in Transforming East and Southeast Asia ... Main Trends of Russia’s Foreign Policy in Transforming East and Southeast Asia Main Trends of Russia’s Foreign Policy in Transforming East and Southeast Asia Changing calculations in East Asia The Vietnam question Developments in Russia’s foreign policy Vladimir N. Kolotov  ... According to official statistics, under Yeltsin Russia’s GDP declined by roughly 60% – which is unprecedented in peace time – with all the ensuing consequences. Russia became weak and concentrated upon domestic problems, and by 1998 it had lost its erstwhile role and almost all influence in East Asia. Under Vladimir Putin, Russia managed to match its own GDP of 1990 only in 2006! Putin proved effective at stopping political and economic chaos, soothing armed conflicts inside Russia, and restoring a level of social and economic development. As a result, Russia has returned to the world stage as a strong state. It is expected that new president Dmitry Medvedev will continue that political course and that the energy dimension will become more considerable in Russia’s foreign policy as President Medvedev previously served as Chairman of the Board of Directors of Gazprom. Changing calculations in East Asia During Russia’s decline, the situation in East Asia, including Southeast Asia, changed a lot. First of all, the region as a whole has undergone rapid economic development and is transforming in a China-centric way. There are several important reasons for the increase of Chinese influence over the last 10 years. Most generally, China has shown impressive economic growth, and the regional balance of power has essentially shifted in China’s favor.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"19/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_35_64318028#1_134618485","relevance":2,"reason":"The full document is focused on Russia's foreign policy in East and Southeast Asia and states that under Yeltsin Russia became weak, inward-looking, and lost almost all influence in East Asia. This directly supports an effect on a region including non-aligned countries such as Vietnam, but it is not specifically dedicated to non-aligned countries as a group.","learned":"Yeltsin-era Russia's domestic economic collapse and inward focus reduced Russian influence in East/Southeast Asia; Vietnam-focused evidence may yield stronger results."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ yeltsin vietnam (+ \"cam ranh\" aid debt influence))","reason":"Vietnam was a non-aligned country and former Soviet partner; searching Yeltsin with Vietnam, Cam Ranh, aid/debt, and influence may uncover concrete effects of Yeltsin-era Russian retrenchment."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ yeltsin vietnam (+ \"cam ranh\" aid debt influence))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_32_405566791#14_770195805","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q913","rank":1,"snippet":"️ Timeline of inventions | Recent history… \" , \"segment\": \"Traditionalists Maturists Veterans Lucky Few Builders (Australia). 1925-1945 (75-95 years old today) World War II (1938-1945) Great depression (1930s) Rationing Fixed gender roles Jobs for life Wrote letters. So called because they generally conformed to the social norms of the time and focused on their careers. The oldest members of the silent generation may have fought in World War II Younger members may have fought in the Korean War and/or <cover>Vietnam War Hardship during the great depression led to the second world war which resulted in fewer children called “the lucky few” Popular Music: Jazz, Swing People born during the silent generation had a huge influence on younger generations: Political leaders: Robert F. Kennedy, Mikhail Gorbachev, Margaret Thatcher and Boris Yeltsin</cover>Revolutionary leaders: Fidel Castro and Che Guevara Civil rights leaders: Martin Luther King and Malcolm X Activists: Gloria Steinem (1934) Writers:\" , \"start_char\": 7718.000000 , \"title\": \"≡ Generation Names List & FAQs. Best 7 Generations Explained\" , \"url\": \"https://www.adducation.info/lifestyle/generation-names/\" }"}}
