{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"non aligned movement\" (+ lost declined relevance importance) \"cold war\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_621764772#9_697691827","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q916","rank":1,"snippet":"quiet and inactive, and in 1983 leadership passed to India, which had abstained on the UN vote, though India maintained close ties with the Soviet Union and with the Soviet puppet regime in Kabul. Cuba lost its bid to become a member of the United Nations Security Council and its ambitions for a role in global leadership had totally collapsed. More broadly the Movement was deeply split over the Soviet–Afghan War in 1979. Although Moscow's allies supported the Soviet intervention, other members of the movement (particularly predominantly Muslim states) condemned it. Post-Cold War Because the <cover>Non-Aligned Movement was formed as an attempt to thaw out the Cold War, it has struggled to find relevance </cover>since the Cold War ended. After the breakup of Yugoslavia, a founding member, its membership was suspended in 1992 at the regular Ministerial Meeting of the Movement, held in New York during the regular yearly session of the General Assembly of the United Nations.\" , \"start_char\": 8371.000000 , \"title\": \"Non-Aligned Movement - Wikipedia\" , \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-Aligned_Movement\" }"}}
