{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_621764772#9_697691827","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage is from a document dedicated to the Non-Aligned Movement and directly states the key post-Cold War effect: because NAM was formed to ease Cold War tensions, it struggled to find relevance after the Cold War ended. This directly supports how non-aligned countries/their movement were affected by the Yeltsin-era Soviet collapse, even though Yeltsin is not named in the snippet.","learned":"A central effect was that after the Cold War and Soviet breakup, the Non-Aligned Movement struggled to find relevance; this is likely the broader consequence for non-aligned countries."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_621764772#9_697691827","reason":"Fetch the full NAM document to confirm the post-Cold-War relevance evidence and possibly find more direct context about Soviet breakup/Yeltsin-era effects."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_621764772#9_697691827"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_621764772#9_697691827","document":"Non-Aligned Movement - Wikipedia ... Non-Aligned Movement Non-Aligned Movement Contents History Origins and the Cold War Cuba's Role Post-Cold War Organizational structure and membership Policies and ideology Current activities and positions Criticism of US policy Self-determination of Puerto Rico Self-determination of Western Sahara Sustainable development Reforms of the UN South–South cooperation Cultural diversity and human rights Summits Chairperson Members, observers and guests Current members Africa Americas Asia Europe Oceania Former members Observers Countries Organisations Guests See also References Further reading External links  ... At the time, Afghanistan was also an active member of the Nonaligned Movement. At the United Nations, Nonaligned members voted 56 to 9, with 26 abstaining, to condemn the Soviet Union. Cuba in fact was deeply in debt financially and politically to Moscow and voted against the resolution. It lost its reputation as nonaligned in the Cold War. Castro, instead of becoming a high-profile spokesman for the Movement, remained quiet and inactive, and in 1983 leadership passed to India, which had abstained on the UN vote, though India maintained close ties with the Soviet Union and with the Soviet puppet regime in Kabul. Cuba lost its bid to become a member of the United Nations Security Council and its ambitions for a role in global leadership had totally collapsed. More broadly the Movement was deeply split over the Soviet–Afghan War in 1979. Although Moscow's allies supported the Soviet intervention, other members of the movement (particularly predominantly Muslim states) condemned it. Post-Cold War Because the Non-Aligned Movement was formed as an attempt to thaw out the Cold War, it has struggled to find relevance since the Cold War ended. After the breakup of Yugoslavia, a founding member, its membership was suspended in 1992 at the regular Ministerial Meeting of the Movement, held in New York during the regular yearly session of the General Assembly of the United Nations.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
