{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_621764772#9_697691827","relevance":3,"reason":"The full document is dedicated to the Non-Aligned Movement and directly states that after the Cold War ended the movement struggled to find relevance, with additional context about Soviet-aligned divisions. This directly supports the main effect on non-aligned countries/their movement from the Yeltsin-era post-Soviet transition.","learned":"The strongest general answer is that the end of the Cold War/Soviet breakup left the Non-Aligned Movement struggling for relevance and exposed divisions among members."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"struggled to find relevance\" \"non-aligned movement\")","reason":"Search the distinctive direct-answer phrase to find other documents or copies focused on the Non-Aligned Movement's post-Cold-War loss of relevance."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ \"struggled to find relevance\" \"non-aligned movement\")"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_621764772#10_697694105","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q917","rank":2,"snippet":"members Africa Americas Asia Europe Oceania Former members Observers Countries Organisations Guests See also References Further reading External links \" , \"segment\": \"Cuba lost its bid to become a member of the United Nations Security Council and its ambitions for a role in global leadership had totally collapsed. More broadly the Movement was deeply split over the Soviet–Afghan War in 1979. Although Moscow's allies supported the Soviet intervention, other members of the movement (particularly predominantly Muslim states) condemned it. Post-Cold War Because the <cover>Non-Aligned Movement was formed as an attempt to thaw out the Cold War, it has struggled to find relevance </cover>since the Cold War ended. After the breakup of Yugoslavia, a founding member, its membership was suspended in 1992 at the regular Ministerial Meeting of the Movement, held in New York during the regular yearly session of the General Assembly of the United Nations. The successor states of the Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia have expressed little interest in membership, though Serbia and Bosnia and Herzegovina have observer status. In 2004, Malta and Cyprus ceased to be members and joined\" , \"start_char\": 8991.000000","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_621764772#9_697691827","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
