{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1450945554#10_3003043202","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage is from a document dedicated to non-alignment/NAM and includes sections on NAM's role in the 1990s, the end of the Cold War, end of bipolarism, economic order, and dilemmas. However, the snippet is mostly a table of contents and does not itself state how Yeltsin or the Soviet collapse affected non-aligned countries.","learned":"Dedicated non-alignment/NAM educational pages with sections on the 1990s and end of bipolarism may contain direct evidence on post-Cold-War effects, so this result is worth opening."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1450945554#10_3003043202","reason":"The document appears dedicated to non-alignment and has promising sections on the 1990s, end of Cold War, end of bipolarism, and dilemmas that may directly answer how non-aligned countries were affected."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1450945554#10_3003043202"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1450945554#10_3003043202","document":"Non-alignment Movement - Political Science ... Non-alignment Movement Non-alignment Movement Meaning of Non-Alignment: The criteria of non-alignment determined as early as June 1961 at Cairo were: Characteristics of Non-Alignment: The conceptual imperatives and major features of non-alignment can be enumerated as below: 1. Averse to Military Alliances: 2. Averse to Cold War: 3. Averse to Ideological Polarization: 4. Own Path of Development: 5. Revolutionary Outlook: 6. Friendship and Equality: 7. Support to U.N.O: 8. Able Leadership: 9. Doubtful Genuineness: 10. Alignments within Non-alignment : 11. Not “Double Alignment”: 12. Growing Institutionalization: Bases and Causes of Non-Alignment: Negative Basis: 1. Dissolution with Military Alliances: Positive Bases: Non-alignment has many positive bases also Which are explained as under: 2. Ideological Basis: 3. Independent Foreign Policy: 4. Economic Basis: 5. Strengthening of U.N.O: Growth and role of NAM: NAM Conferences: Summit Conferences of NAM: Role in the 50s and 60s: Role in the 70s: Role in the 80s: Role in the 90s: Critical Evaluation of Non-Alignment: Achievements of non-alignment and their impact on international relations are summarized as below: 1. World Peace: 2. End of Cold War: 3. End of Bipolarism: 4. End of Colonialism: 5. End of Racialism: 6. End of Military Alliances: 7. Minimized the arms race: 8. Preservation of Independence: 9. Strengthened U.N.O: 10. Universal International System: 11. More Economic Aid: 12. New Economic Order: 13. New Communication Order: Criticism: 1. Ambiguous Concept: 2. Not a Model of Behavior: 3. Selfish Policy: 4. Opportunistic and Immoral: 5. Bloc Mentality: 6. Harsh towards West: 7. Disunity: 8. Serious Dilemma: 9 Hypocrisy: 10. Labour Union Approach: 11 . Factionalism: 12. Like Alliance System: 13. Worthless Declarations and Conferences: 14. No Results: The relevance of Non-Alignment: Irrelevant and Invalid : Still Relevant and Valid: REFERENCES: Share this:  ... It should not be a member of multilateral military alliances concluded in the context of Great Power conflicts. If it has conceded military bases, these concessions should not have been made in the context of Great Power conflicts. If it is a member of a bilateral or regional defense arrangement, this should not be in the context of Great Power conflicts. The disintegration of alliance systems in recent years is indeed a vindication of the non-aligned position. After disintegration, the nonaligned movement (NAM) is increasingly concentrating on economic issues and emancipation. Characteristics of Non-Alignment: The conceptual imperatives and major features of non-alignment can be enumerated as below: 1. Averse to Military Alliances: Non-alignment opposed military alliances of all types like NATO, SEATO, CENTO, Warsaw Pact, etc.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
