{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1450945554#7_3003032844","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage is from a document dedicated to non-alignment/NAM and lists effects such as the end of bipolarism, end of military alliances, more economic aid, and a new economic order. However, the snippet is largely a table of contents and does not itself directly explain how the Yeltsin-era Soviet collapse affected non-aligned countries.","learned":"The 'end of bipolarism' framing is promising because it matches the strategic change that affected NAM after the Soviet/Yeltsin-era transition."},"action":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1450945554#7_3003032844","reason":"Open this dedicated non-alignment document segment because it may contain direct text explaining the post-bipolar/post-Soviet effects on NAM and non-aligned countries."}}
{"request":{"op":"document","docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1450945554#7_3003032844"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1450945554#7_3003032844","document":"Non-alignment Movement - Political Science ... Non-alignment Movement Non-alignment Movement Meaning of Non-Alignment: The criteria of non-alignment determined as early as June 1961 at Cairo were: Characteristics of Non-Alignment: The conceptual imperatives and major features of non-alignment can be enumerated as below: 1. Averse to Military Alliances: 2. Averse to Cold War: 3. Averse to Ideological Polarization: 4. Own Path of Development: 5. Revolutionary Outlook: 6. Friendship and Equality: 7. Support to U.N.O: 8. Able Leadership: 9. Doubtful Genuineness: 10. Alignments within Non-alignment : 11. Not “Double Alignment”: 12. Growing Institutionalization: Bases and Causes of Non-Alignment: Negative Basis: 1. Dissolution with Military Alliances: Positive Bases: Non-alignment has many positive bases also Which are explained as under: 2. Ideological Basis: 3. Independent Foreign Policy: 4. Economic Basis: 5. Strengthening of U.N.O: Growth and role of NAM: NAM Conferences: Summit Conferences of NAM: Role in the 50s and 60s: Role in the 70s: Role in the 80s: Role in the 90s: Critical Evaluation of Non-Alignment: Achievements of non-alignment and their impact on international relations are summarized as below: 1. World Peace: 2. End of Cold War: 3. End of Bipolarism: 4. End of Colonialism: 5. End of Racialism: 6. End of Military Alliances: 7. Minimized the arms race: 8. Preservation of Independence: 9. Strengthened U.N.O: 10. Universal International System: 11. More Economic Aid: 12. New Economic Order: 13. New Communication Order: Criticism: 1. Ambiguous Concept: 2. Not a Model of Behavior: 3. Selfish Policy: 4. Opportunistic and Immoral: 5. Bloc Mentality: 6. Harsh towards West: 7. Disunity: 8. Serious Dilemma: 9 Hypocrisy: 10. Labour Union Approach: 11 . Factionalism: 12. Like Alliance System: 13. Worthless Declarations and Conferences: 14. No Results: The relevance of Non-Alignment: Irrelevant and Invalid : Still Relevant and Valid: REFERENCES: Share this:  ... As a positive concept, it has several dimensions; naturally, non-alignment should oppose certain values and at the same time promote some others which are in harmony with its basic orientation. The chief goals of the non-aligned movement in the fifties and sixties re decolonize and preserve international peace. Of late, it has been contributing positively to attaining a new international economic order and a new information order based on equity, justice, freedom, and the eradication of exploitation and domination. As an activist and dynamic policy, it takes Specific sides on the merit of each case. This implies that issue-bound tilts in nonalignment are considered legitimate, and the concept does not imply equidistant from both the superpowers. But at the same time, it also rejects the idea of natural allies recently coined to justify certain alliances of the non-aligned states with certain powers. Thus, it is an active policy as it envisages an active role for the non-aligned countries in world affairs. It is positive as well since it also strives for certain values and goals. Zambian President Kenneth Kaunda elaborated on the goodness of this concept in 1964.","ok":true,"op":"document"}}
